{
  "dom_id": "step-q143476",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{90}$ be a random sample of size $n=90$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =92$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =30.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $90$ and $95.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=90,\\,\\mu =92,\\,\\sigma =30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(92,\\frac{{30}^{2}}{90}) \\\\  & ∼N(92,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =P(\\frac{90-92}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{95-92}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.63<Z<0.95) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.95)-P(Z<-0.63) \\\\  & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.95) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ(0.63) & =0.7357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, by the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.63) & =1-Φ(0.63) \\\\  & =1-0.7357 \\\\  & =0.2643.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63) \\\\  & =0.8289-0.2643 \\\\  & =0.5646.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
